# React Refs: Understanding the Mechanics

`useRef` does two things that look unrelated: it holds a value across renders without causing one, and it gives you the real DOM node for an element. In the video, we built it into freact, [our fake React from Module 1](<../Module 1 - Manipulating the DOM/13 - React Components Understanding the Mechanics Part I.webnb>), and the build shows that they are the same feature.

The updated project is in this module's `freact/05-refs/` folder, next to this notebook. It picks up exactly where Module 1's `freact/04-state/` left off, and the additions are small: one new hook function and one new branch in the props loop. To watch it run, right-click `index.html` in that folder, choose "Show Preview", and open the browser console.

## Another Slot in the Same Array

You saw where state lives in ["React State: Understanding the Mechanics"](<../Module 1 - Manipulating the DOM/22 - React State Understanding the Mechanics.webnb#where-the-value-actually-lives>): a `hooks` array outside every component, one slot per hook call, claimed in call order by a counter that resets each render.

Refs go in that same array. There is no second system.

```{walkthrough}
title: useRef, whole
step: One array for every kind of hook
file: freact/05-refs/freact.js
region: hooks
The same two variables from the state reading. The only thing that changed is the comment: slots now hold state values *or* ref boxes, depending on which hook claimed them.
@"let hooks": A slot's meaning comes entirely from which call claimed it, which is one more reason hook order must never change between renders.

step: The whole hook
file: freact/05-refs/freact.js
region: use-ref
Claim the next slot; on the first render, put an object there; return that object. That is the entire hook.
@"hooks[idx] = { current": The box, created once. `{ current: initialVal }` is all a ref is.
@"return hooks[idx]": Every render after the first finds the slot occupied and returns the **same object**. Your component gets back the identical box each time, which is what lets an assignment to `.current` survive from one render to the next.
```

Compare it with `useState` and two absences stand out.

**There is no setter.** You change a ref by assigning to `.current` yourself.

**Nothing schedules a render.** `useState`'s setter compares, stores, and calls for a re-render. `useRef` does none of that, which is exactly why changing a ref does not update the screen.

That also explains why the box exists at all. If `useRef` returned the value directly, there would be no way to change it in a way the next render could see: the next render would call `useRef` again and get whatever was in the slot. Returning the same **object** every time means `ref.current = 5` writes into something that survives.

## How a Ref Reaches a DOM Node

`ref={inputRef}` looks like an attribute, and it travels like one: it is a prop, carried in the element description, holding the ref object. What is special is what `_createDomNode` does when it finds it.

```{walkthrough}
title: The ref prop
step: One new branch in the props loop
file: freact/05-refs/freact.js
region: ref-prop
Between `className` and the ordinary attributes, one new case. No attribute is written; the prop is consumed, like an event prop.
@"propVal.current = dom": The node was created two lines up, and the ref that held `null` now points at it. One assignment is the entire connection between `useRef` in your component and the element on the page.

step: Why the ref is null during render
file: freact/05-refs/freact.js
region: function-component
Look at the order of operations. Your component function runs here, at the top of `_createDomNode` -- and the `input` it describes is created further down, on a later recursive call.
@"element.type(element.props)": When this call runs, `useRef` hands back the box, and the box still holds `null`: no DOM node below this element exists yet. A ref read during render is a ref read too early. By the time an event handler runs, the nodes exist and the box has been filled in.
```

## The Component

```{walkthrough}
title: An input, a ref, and a caveat
file: freact/05-refs/App.jsx
The component from the video: an input that carries the ref, and a button whose handler reads it.
@"useRef(null)": `null` until some node claims the ref, which is why `null` is the conventional starting value for a DOM ref.
@"ref={inputRef}": The prop that triggers the new branch. When this input is built, the box gets filled.
@"console.log(inputRef.current)": Runs when you click, long after the render. Preview the page, open the console, click the button, and the log shows the real `input` element.
@"// setClickCount(clickCount": Commented out on purpose. Uncomment it, save, reload the preview, type something into the input, and click: the count goes up and *what you typed disappears*.
```

(While you are looking at the button, notice that it renders a count of `0` correctly. That is the `??` fix from the state reading still at work: the text branch reads `nodeValue ?? ''`, because `||` would have swallowed the falsy `0` into an empty string.)

That disappearing text is the caveat from the video, and it is worth understanding rather than memorizing. Our freact rebuilds the entire DOM on every render, so the re-render throws the old input away, node and contents, and makes a brand new one; the ref is then pointed at the new node. Real React does not do this: it keeps the existing DOM nodes and updates them in place, so typed text survives a re-render and a ref stays valid across renders. That difference is the part of React we have not built, and it is why the real thing is more than a few dozen lines.

## The Same in Real React

```{react autorun}
function FocusInput() {
  const inputRef = React.useRef(null);

  function focusInput() {
    inputRef.current.focus();
  }

  return (
    <section>
      <input aria-label="Name" ref={inputRef} />
      <button onClick={focusInput}>Focus input</button>
    </section>
  );
}

renderReact(<FocusInput />);
```

No `querySelector`, no `id`. The ref points at this component's own input, which keeps working when the component is rendered twice on one page.

## Exercises

1. Write `useRef`. It should claim a slot, create the box on the first call only, and return the same object every time.

    ```{javascript}
    const hooks = [];
    let hookIndex = 0;

    function useRef(initialVal) {
      // TODO: take the current index and move hookIndex on by one.
      // If the slot is empty, put { current: initialVal } in it.
      // Return the object in the slot.
    }

    hookIndex = 0;
    const first = useRef(null);

    first.current = 'a DOM node';

    // A second render calls the same hook again.
    hookIndex = 0;
    const second = useRef(null);

    console.log(second.current, first === second);
    ```
    ```+solution
    const hooks = [];
    let hookIndex = 0;

    function useRef(initialVal) {
      const idx = hookIndex;

      hookIndex = hookIndex + 1;

      if (hooks[idx] === undefined) {
        hooks[idx] = { current: initialVal };
      }

      return hooks[idx];
    }

    hookIndex = 0;
    const first = useRef(null);

    first.current = 'a DOM node';

    // A second render calls the same hook again.
    hookIndex = 0;
    const second = useRef(null);

    console.log(second.current, first === second);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(first === second).should('equal', true).run('Both renders should get the very same object back, which is what lets `.current` survive', 'Returned the same object');
    wrap(second.current).should('equal', 'a DOM node').run('The value written to `.current` should still be there on the next render', 'The value survived the render');
    wrap(hooks.length).should('equal', 1).run('Two renders of one `useRef` call should use one slot, not two. Claim `hooks[idx]` rather than pushing a new entry', 'Used a single slot');
    ```

2. Complete the `ref` branch so the created node is written into the ref instead of being set as an attribute. (This cell reaches the real document as `window.document`, since a cell's own `document` has no `createElement`.)

    ```{javascript}
    const realDocument = window.document;

    const inputRef = { current: null };

    const element = {
      type: 'input',
      props: { id: 'name', ref: inputRef, children: [] }
    };

    const dom = realDocument.createElement(element.type);

    for (const propName in element.props) {
      if (propName === 'children') {
        continue;
      } else if (propName === 'ref') {
        // TODO: point the ref's current at the dom node
      } else {
        dom.setAttribute(propName, element.props[propName]);
      }
    }

    console.log(inputRef.current ? inputRef.current.tagName : 'still null');
    ```
    ```+solution
    const realDocument = window.document;

    const inputRef = { current: null };

    const element = {
      type: 'input',
      props: { id: 'name', ref: inputRef, children: [] }
    };

    const dom = realDocument.createElement(element.type);

    for (const propName in element.props) {
      if (propName === 'children') {
        continue;
      } else if (propName === 'ref') {
        element.props[propName].current = dom;
      } else {
        dom.setAttribute(propName, element.props[propName]);
      }
    }

    console.log(inputRef.current ? inputRef.current.tagName : 'still null');
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(inputRef.current === dom).should('equal', true).run('Set the ref`s `current` to the node that was just created', 'Pointed the ref at the node');
    wrap(dom.getAttribute('id')).should('equal', 'name').run('Other props should still become attributes', 'Set the ordinary attribute');
    wrap(dom.hasAttribute('ref')).should('equal', false).run('`ref` should not be written to the DOM as an attribute', 'Did not set a ref attribute');
    ```

3. Why a box and not the value

    ```{mcq}
    `useRef` returns an object with a `current` property instead of the value itself. What would break if it returned the value directly?
    correct: Correct! Returning the same object each render is what lets an assignment to `.current` survive. If the value itself came back, assigning to your local variable would change nothing that the next render could see.
    wrong: Compare `useRef` with `useState` in the walkthrough: `useRef` has no setter, so something else has to make a change stick.
    [x] Assigning to a returned value would not persist; assigning into a shared object does
    [ ] React requires every hook to return an object
    [ ] The object is what triggers the re-render
    [ ] `current` is needed so the value can be garbage collected
    ```

4. The null during render

    ```{mcq}
    A component logs `inputRef.current` in its body, during render, and sees `null`. Why?
    correct: Correct! The component runs first and produces a description; only afterwards does `_createDomNode` create nodes and fill in the refs. Reading the ref during render happens before that has taken place.
    wrong: Think about the order: when does your function run, and when does the DOM node come into existence? Look at the second step of "The ref prop" walkthrough.
    [x] The node has not been created yet when the component function runs
    [ ] Refs are only readable inside effects, by design
    [ ] The ref was not attached, so it stays `null` forever
    [ ] React clears every ref at the start of each render
    ```

